WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.460
This video is going to bit different to my
usually videos, but I want to talk about material

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:08.160
properties and the words that we use to describe
them, this is information you are going to

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.450
need to fully understand future videos.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:15.299
By the end of this video I want you to understand
6 key words we use to describe material.

00:00:15.299 --> 00:00:19.119
These are stiff, strong, ductile, brittle,
tough and hard.

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:22.990
With this words you will be able to describe
pretty much any material and better understand

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:26.090
why certain materials are used in different
applications.

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:29.829
More technical videos like this will be uploaded
to my second channel from now on, which you

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:36.040
can subscribe to by following this link.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.180
First we are going to learn what a tensile
test is.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:41.520
A tensile test is a fundamental test in material
mechanics.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.130
It’s performed by pulling a sample of material
apart until failure, while measuring the force

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:47.500
and displacement.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:53.300
It provides us with something called a stress/strain
curve.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:57.620
In this scenario the stress is defined by
the force applied to the test sample divided

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.460
by the cross-sectional area.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:03.620
This gives us units of Newtons per metre squared,
which you may recognise as the metric unit

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.780
for pressure Pascals.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.460
Stress goes on the Y-Axis.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.500
Strain describes how much deformation has
occurred with that applied force and it is

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:14.890
found by dividing the change in length by
the original length.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:16.670
This is placed this on the x axis.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:20.880
Let’s watch this test again and see what
information we can get from the stress/strain

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:21.880
graph.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.990
As the stress rises the material begins to
deform, this initial linear region is elastic

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:27.250
deformation.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:31.290
That means that if we remove the force the
material will regain its original shape, think

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:35.330
of how a rubber band can be deform hugely
and still come back to it’s original shape.

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:39.760
The end of this linear elastic deformation
is marked by the yield point, from here out

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.850
any additional stress will cause permanent
deformation.

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:44.710
This is called plastic deformation.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:48.549
The stress continues to rise until it hits
the ultimate tensile strength point.

00:01:48.549 --> 00:01:52.510
This is the ultimate strength of the material,
the most stress it can handle.

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:56.850
From here less stress is needed as the material
begins to decrease in cross section, which

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.460
you can see happening here, this is called
necking.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.930
This continues until the material fractures.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:06.720
We can get a lot of really useful information
from this graph, the first is Young’s Modulus,

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.319
otherwise known as the elastic modulus.

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:12.790
This describes how stiff the material is and
it is obtained by finding the slope of this

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:13.790
linear region.

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:18.150
A steeper slope means a stiffer material,
for example a high carbon steel may look like

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.329
this.

00:02:19.329 --> 00:02:23.519
Whereas a flexible material with a low Young’s
modulus, like rubber would look like this.

00:02:23.519 --> 00:02:27.019
This graph is not to scale, but it should
give you an idea of how this information is

00:02:27.019 --> 00:02:28.019
represented.

00:02:28.019 --> 00:02:31.569
Young’s modulus is one of the most used
properties in engineering as we can use it

00:02:31.569 --> 00:02:34.700
to predict deflection in a huge range of scenarios.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:38.219
Yield strength and ultimate tensile strength
are two other important properties.

00:02:38.219 --> 00:02:42.219
An engineer will divide the yield or ultimate
strength by the safety factor to achieve the

00:02:42.219 --> 00:02:46.609
max allowable stress, which is used to influence
the design of the product.

00:02:46.609 --> 00:02:50.999
Usually engineers will aim to keep the max
possible stress well below failure, but safety

00:02:50.999 --> 00:02:52.909
factors differ between industries.

00:02:52.909 --> 00:02:56.010
So we have seen a stiff material and a flexible
material.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:00.480
Now let’s look at a material in between,
this material can be described as tough and

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:01.480
ductile.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:05.159
Tough simple means the material can absorb
a lot of energy without breaking.

00:03:05.159 --> 00:03:09.030
The area under the graph here defines how
much energy is absorbed.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:10.760
Ductile means it deforms under pressure.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:14.069
The two previous materials could also be considered
ductile.

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:18.189
Spring steel is a tough and ductile material,
with a high yield strength, which is why it

00:03:18.189 --> 00:03:19.909
is used in springs.

00:03:19.909 --> 00:03:22.819
Springs need to absorb and release energy
without permanently deforming.

00:03:22.819 --> 00:03:24.469
The opposite of ductile is brittle.

00:03:24.469 --> 00:03:28.340
A brittle material is a material that breaks
with very little deformation.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:31.900
Glass, ceramics and cast iron all fall into
this category.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:36.109
You can actually tell if a material is brittle
or ductile by examining the fracture surface

00:03:36.109 --> 00:03:37.360
after they have broken.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:41.559
A ductile material will have this characteristic
cup and cone fracture surface, whereas a brittle

00:03:41.559 --> 00:03:44.669
fractures have granular flat looking fracture
surfaces.

00:03:44.669 --> 00:03:48.079
Some materials can go from ductile to brittle
when their temperature is lowered.

00:03:48.079 --> 00:03:51.959
This was actually a massive problem during
world war two with the liberty ship.

00:03:51.959 --> 00:03:56.459
Several of these ships literally broke in
half with no warning, including the SS John

00:03:56.459 --> 00:03:59.849
P. Gaines, which broke in half in the frigid
waters of the bering sea.

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:04.349
It was later discovered that the grade of
steel being used became brittle at lower temperatures.

00:04:04.349 --> 00:04:08.360
This problem was made worse by stress concentration
at the hatches, which you learned about in

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.970
my first video “Why are plane windows round”.

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:14.590
This embrittlement is also thought to have
also contributed to the fracture of the Titanic's

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:15.590
hull.

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:18.650
The final material property I want to talk
about is hardness.

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:21.959
It is directly related to the stiffness and
yield strength of the material.

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:26.479
But it is used to describe how difficult it
is to dent, scratch and abrade materials.

00:04:26.479 --> 00:04:30.610
One way this material property is measured
is with the rockwell hardness test.

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:35.889
This test involves three steps, first a minor
load is applied to the material by an indenter.

00:04:35.889 --> 00:04:37.990
This establishes a zero point.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:42.780
For the second step a major load is then added
which indents the material and for the final

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:46.940
step the major load is removed while maintaining
the initial load.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:50.400
The difference in depth between the first
and third step is then used to calculate the

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.129
hardness of the material.

00:04:52.129 --> 00:04:56.099
Diamond is a very hard material, which is
why it is used in cutting tools.

00:04:56.099 --> 00:05:00.370
One of fantastic properties of steel is it’s
ability to be heat treated to have it’s

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:02.240
hardness tailored in different areas.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.759
For example with swords you want your cutting
edge to be hard, but the core of the blade

00:05:05.759 --> 00:05:06.919
to be ductile.

00:05:06.919 --> 00:05:10.389
This means the sword can bend under pressure
without breaking, while the cutting edge can

00:05:10.389 --> 00:05:11.660
resist damage.

